==============================================================================
[3] VERDICT
==============================================================================
  P1 masks confound fuel with wind SPEED (but do give wind DIRECTION)
     weak direction is the R0/U/k_LB trade-off (load 1.73): -0.60*lb_k  +0.60*U  -0.53*R0   -> OK
     early-window CRB(U) = 6.92 (above the 10% tolerance)   -> OK
     degeneracy is a function of burn development: 1x worse at 9 min than at 27.5 min   -> NO
     MLE error on U = 26% while theta_w is only 0.07 deg   -> OK

  P2 the plume image breaks that confound (smoke is advected by wind,
     independently of fuel)
     CRB(U) at full window: 3.5531 -> 0.00161   (2205x)
     CRB(U) at 9 min:       6.92 -> 0.00077   (8991x)  <- the window that matters
     MLE error on U: 26% -> 1.3%  (20.6x)   -> OK

  P3 the air-quality point supplies emission strength Q only when the
     camera cannot -- its value is conditional on the image not saturating
     saturated image  (47% of pixels at the ceiling): adding the sensor improves CRB(Q) 3.5x   -> OK
     calibrated image (0% at the ceiling): adding the sensor improves CRB(Q) 1.0x   -> OK
     (MLE error on Q with mask+plume alone: 0.49%)

  P4 the practical consequence, in minutes
     wind 2 m/s: U known to 10% at  never (>27 min) with masks, 2.2 min with the plume  -> 24.8 min gained
     wind 4 m/s: U known to 10% at         19.8 min with masks, 2.2 min with the plume  -> 17.5 min gained
     wind 6 m/s: U known to 10% at         13.6 min with masks, 2.2 min with the plume  -> 11.4 min gained
     -> OK

  (context) mask-only CRB on smoke terms: Q=3.8e+02  w_buoy=3.8e+02  -- masks carry no information about either

